WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.740
- I have to apologize.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.850
I ran late today.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.920
I was perfectly fine coming downstairs

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.780
and was like, I felt funky,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.830
so I sprayed perfume.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.425
- [Rob] In your eye?

00:00:09.425 --> 00:00:11.130
- And, sprayed it directly in my eyeball.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.003
And, it's not good.

00:00:14.003 --> 00:00:15.831
- Gee, your eye smells delicious.

00:00:15.831 --> 00:00:18.060
- I know, it's burns so badly.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:18.893
Have you ever?

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:19.920
I've never done that.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:20.753
- Oh yeah, I did.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:21.960
I was doing a play in London once

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.140
and they were doing my hair
and somebody called me.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.597
I turned around and got
a full shot of hairspray

00:00:27.597 --> 00:00:28.861
in both eyes.

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:29.694
- Oh.
- Not good.

00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:30.900
- I gotta be honest, I've had hairspray

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.090
because of that kind of similar situation.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.803
Perfume sucks.
- Is way worse.

00:00:35.659 --> 00:00:37.230
You look though, I don't
notice a difference.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.342
You look great, she looks great.

00:00:38.342 --> 00:00:40.170
- Do you wanna smell me?

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.060
- That's a sexy eye.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.500
- You've done a lot of interviews.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.880
I mean, God for so many years.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.440
So what, do you remember your first one?

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.060
I think you were really young, right?

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.590
- Yeah, oh, I remember my first one

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.390
just because I still
laugh at the headline.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.150
It was in the Dayton Daily News in Ohio.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.590
I was living in Ohio.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:56.790
And how about this?

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.623
Weird hell.

00:00:57.623 --> 00:00:59.660
- [Kelly] Oh, you're so cute.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.750
- Oh, there is is.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.810
Boy, 12, sets his cap for stardom.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.340
- Sets his cap for stardom?

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.140
- I just set my cap and off I went.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.670
- It sounds straight up out of the 50s.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:09.540
- Doesn't it?

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.890
It's like really insane.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.900
- Sets his cap, gee, golly.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.490
- I know, I was so proud of that.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.720
You would've thought that was the cover

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.370
of Time Magazine for me.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.395
- Oh no, I wasn't that young,

00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:20.460
but I did like, I don't know,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.110
like junior high do something for singing

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.060
and you'd thought I'd won a Grammy.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:24.893
- [Rob] Oh, for sure.

00:01:24.893 --> 00:01:26.520
- I was in the Burleson Star

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.920
and I was like, I've made it.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.820
- And, I kept it.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.967
My parents have 5,000 copies of it.

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:33.570
- That's awesome though.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:34.950
It's like the first thing you do

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.540
that inspired you to keep going.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.373
It's fun.

00:01:37.373 --> 00:01:38.474
- It's really fun.

00:01:38.474 --> 00:01:39.307
- You knew you wanted to
be an actor very young.

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:40.320
I didn't know I wanted to be a singer

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.670
until I was teenager.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.650
- I was eight when I knew.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.360
Just because I went and saw a
local in Dayton, production,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.370
like literally a neighborhood
playhouse production of Oliver

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:51.810
and there were kids in it.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.060
And, like the light went off for me

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.780
and I knew that but at
a serious, serious level

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.900
where all the other kids
thought I was like a freak

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.610
'cause I was dead serious.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.450
- Well, you have kids and I have kids.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.287
I have an eight year
old actually right now

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:06.120
and I can't imagine her just being like,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:06.957
this is it right now.

00:02:06.957 --> 00:02:07.890
- [Rob] Can you imagine?

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.121
- No.

00:02:09.121 --> 00:02:09.954
- You'd be like,

00:02:09.954 --> 00:02:10.787
"Honey, you have your
whole life ahead of you.

00:02:10.787 --> 00:02:12.480
"You're gonna learn about so many things."

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.313
- But you knew.

00:02:13.313 --> 00:02:14.790
- I was like, shut up,
I'm gonna be an actor.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:15.870
(laughing)

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:16.950
- Bold.
- Yeah.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.953
- So, I hear you had a
memorable moment though

00:02:18.953 --> 00:02:20.370
with a famous musician.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.113
This is the craziest story ever.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.410
So, it's right after you got sober, right?

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.727
- Oh, I know, yes.

00:02:26.727 --> 00:02:29.010
So, on my podcast, Literally,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.450
one of the things that I love

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.290
is I get to not only just talk
to people like Chris Pratt

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.160
and Gwyneth, and Oprah
and just the biggies.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:38.993
But I also get to talk to people

00:02:38.993 --> 00:02:41.310
that I get to nerd out
to that I don't know.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.280
Like Jann Wenner created
the Rolling Stone.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.840
He has a book out that's great.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.260
I had him on and I was
talking about when I got,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.970
we're both sober, Jann and I.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.160
I got sober 32 years ago.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.487
- [Kelly] That's awesome.

00:02:54.487 --> 00:02:55.320
- Thanks.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.397
- That's so awesome, yeah.

00:02:57.397 --> 00:02:58.710
Here's another gee, golly.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.421
- I set my cap and I got sober.

00:03:01.421 --> 00:03:02.820
(laughing)

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:03.653
- [Kelly] Nice callback.

00:03:03.653 --> 00:03:04.699
- That's right, yeah.

00:03:04.699 --> 00:03:07.057
And so, I was newly sober.

00:03:07.057 --> 00:03:10.890
And, I was backstage at
a Rolling Stones concert

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.327
and Keith Richards said,

00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:17.490
"Won't you come back to the
hotel and party with me later?"

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.290
And, I was like,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.260
okay, I'm either gonna
party with Keith Richards

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.850
and not be sober

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.807
or I'm gonna turn down Keith
Richards to go party with?

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.850
I'm 20, whatever it is.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.980
And I remember, I didn't do it,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:32.967
but it was like one of those.

00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:35.430
- Good for you 'cause that's hard

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.330
because you know what though?

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.340
If you turned down something that rad,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.320
you knew that you needed to
make a change in your life.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.280
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.
- You know that,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.706
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:42.706 --> 00:03:44.190
- And, that's actually a good point.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.023
- I felt really good.

00:03:45.023 --> 00:03:45.870
I felt like if I could do that

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.519
then I was gonna be.

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:48.752
(audience applause)

00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:49.770
- I always get worried about stuff.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.090
I've been invited to certain things

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.740
and I'm just not like that rad.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.120
I'm like, I can't hang with people.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.470
So, I'm like, oh, I'm busy.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.340
I always say I'm busy

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.780
'cause I'm nervous in those environments.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.640
- You have celebrity intimidation factor?

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.962
- It's not so much that.

00:04:00.962 --> 00:04:03.930
I think that maybe yes

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.590
because I feel like everybody feels like

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.030
we're all at some barbecue

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.230
and we all know each other

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.390
and we all are supposed to
have everything in common.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.072
And I'm like, what?

00:04:14.072 --> 00:04:16.770
We all have separate friends
outside of this world.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:17.660
I know it's weird.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.493
- No, it's true.

00:04:18.493 --> 00:04:20.914
- And I'm like, we're all
not cool and we're gonna die.

00:04:20.914 --> 00:04:22.320
It's like build a bridge and get over it.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.874
It's like we're all not that cool.

00:04:23.874 --> 00:04:25.359
- Truth.

00:04:25.359 --> 00:04:26.910
- All right, We just saw a clip

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.560
from your new Netflix movie Dog Gone.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.360
So it's based on a true story, right?

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.193
- Yes.

00:04:31.193 --> 00:04:32.430
- And this story breaks my heart

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.600
'cause I have a similar story.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.120
Not completely similar,
but I've lost a dog.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.200
- Ugh, It's the worst.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.550
I mean anybody who has lost,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.110
like, literally, where is my dog?

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.921
It was here a minute
ago and now it's gone.

00:04:41.921 --> 00:04:45.960
It's a true story about a family

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.250
who's having their own issues

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:51.330
and their beloved pet gets
lost on the Appalachian trail

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.640
and father and son who don't get along

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.670
are forced to go on this adventure

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.350
to try to find this dog.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:02.790
And you'll cry your eyes out.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:03.623
- I cry all the time.

00:05:03.623 --> 00:05:08.132
- It's super, it is the
best feel good movie

00:05:08.132 --> 00:05:11.280
I've been a part of in a long, long time.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.640
I'm super happy with it.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.010
- I love that for you.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.090
Just as a project in general,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.634
to feel that kind of like--

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:17.467
- And it's got dogs, come on.

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:19.770
- Yeah, but I also feel

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:20.940
like it's based on a true story, right?

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.430
So it's like I sometimes
feel like life is like that.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.497
Like things happen that are just tragic

00:05:25.497 --> 00:05:29.130
to bring people together because
that is kind of interesting

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.130
that you said the father and
son weren't really relating.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.719
It wasn't adding up for them, right?

00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:36.630
And then by the end I
assume like, you know.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.319
- They understand each other better.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:40.200
And I have to say, because I know

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.940
a lot of people said,
"I wanna see that movie,

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.300
"but I'm not watching
it if that dog dies."

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.130
- [Kelly] Old Yeller,
it's like killed us all.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.120
- Dog does not die.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.460
I know that's like in theory spoiler,

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:51.293
but it isn't.

00:05:51.293 --> 00:05:52.657
I've ran into way too
many people who are like,

00:05:52.657 --> 00:05:55.477
"I'm not watching it
if I even get a whiff."

00:05:55.477 --> 00:05:59.280
- Because we all saw
Old Yeller as children

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.641
and it was dramatic.

00:06:00.641 --> 00:06:02.430
- Oh When The Red Fern Grows.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:03.263
- [Kelly] When The Red Fern,

00:06:03.263 --> 00:06:04.110
or Where The Red Fern Grows.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.220
- Where The Red Fern, woo.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.173
- But when too, both questions.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.780
But do you remember the
first dog you ever had?

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.613
You're a dog person.

00:06:10.613 --> 00:06:11.580
You have like a couple dogs right?

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:12.480
- Yeah, I have four dogs.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.422
- [Kelly] Oh, you have four dogs?

00:06:13.422 --> 00:06:14.255
- I have four dogs.

00:06:14.255 --> 00:06:15.088
- [Kelly] I have two.

00:06:15.088 --> 00:06:16.350
That's a lot, four dogs.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.240
Oh, look at that love.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.080
- [Rob] That's Daisy.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:19.950
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:23.850
- Yeah, that's Owen,
the big dog and Daisy.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.470
And sometimes I just go in

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.390
and I just cuddle up with them.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.390
They come into my bed
sometimes I go to their beds.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.334
- It's interesting though, dogs know.

00:06:32.334 --> 00:06:34.517
I was having like a really hard time

00:06:34.517 --> 00:06:37.920
and I hadn't slept alone in years,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.028
and my dog, who never was
allowed to be in the bed,

00:06:41.028 --> 00:06:42.684
literally crawled up,

00:06:42.684 --> 00:06:44.700
and he like got right beside me

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:45.750
and I just snuggled with him.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.070
And he knew.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.630
That's why I like animals
better than humans,

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:50.463
yeah, most times.

00:06:50.463 --> 00:06:51.296
- I'm down with that.

00:06:51.296 --> 00:06:52.433
- I mean, you're great,
but I just mean like,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.899
but most times animals
are just really awesome.

00:06:55.899 --> 00:06:58.980
You mentioned happy tears
though at the end of Dog Gone,

00:06:58.980 --> 00:06:59.813
Which is awesome.

00:06:59.813 --> 00:07:00.646
We need that.

00:07:00.646 --> 00:07:03.030
So what's the first movie
that made you like ugly cry?

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.070
- Oh, for sure Brian's Song.

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.120
It's going way back.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.038
- Oh my God, I know that.

00:07:07.038 --> 00:07:12.038
- And I have a theory that
every man of a certain age

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.220
without a doubt,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.660
first time they cry was
watching Brian Song.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.385
'Cause it's a manly movie
and it's about football.

00:07:18.385 --> 00:07:20.915
(howling)

00:07:20.915 --> 00:07:23.430
- And that was like, I
think Rudy for me too.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.263
- Oh, Rudy's great.

00:07:24.263 --> 00:07:25.096
- Rudy, yeah at the end.

00:07:25.096 --> 00:07:26.010
I don't know, yeah,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.496
- Rudy is great.

00:07:27.496 --> 00:07:28.329
There's so many.

00:07:28.329 --> 00:07:30.270
I love those kinds of
movies and as you know,

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.800
they don't get made so much anymore.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:32.813
- They don't.

00:07:32.813 --> 00:07:36.960
I don't know, I sometimes feel
like life is serious enough.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.580
I do want to feel good.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.440
So, please make more of these movies.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.090
I like to go to the movie
theater and believe--

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.690
- Everybody watch Dog Gone
and we'll make more of them.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.550
- It is sad though, losing your dog.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.990
I'm like, oh, I love the happy ending,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:53.460
and I love that it's not a spoiler alert.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.900
We need that feel good thing.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.430
But Dog Gone is is also the story

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.570
of a father and son, like you mentioned.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:59.460
So you've got two boys.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.330
Do you guys bond a lot?

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.260
How do you bond?

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.093
- We bond a lot.

00:08:02.093 --> 00:08:02.926
I'm really lucky.

00:08:02.926 --> 00:08:04.146
I'm very close to them.

00:08:04.146 --> 00:08:06.750
We, ski together.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.543
- You're like the coolest
family in the, what the hell?

00:08:08.543 --> 00:08:09.660
- There's my boys.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:10.980
There they are.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.470
- I'm trying to keep up
with 'em, it's tough.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.990
It's tough to keep up with those abs,

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:17.253
but I'm trying.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.242
I have a new show on Netflix
coming we'll talk about,

00:08:23.242 --> 00:08:24.120
but it's a few months off

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:28.200
that I co-created with my son John Owen.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.130
- My son Matthew, he's
in the finance world.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.850
So now I'm really like learning
about their work lives.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:37.179
It's a whole different phase

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:39.870
where you get them through childhood

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.360
then all of a sudden
it's like, how's work?

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.193
It's super cool.

00:08:43.193 --> 00:08:44.640
- I know I have friends
that have older kids

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:45.860
and it's just like,

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.040
it almost becomes like a
different kind of friendship

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.030
with your kid.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.500
Like, because they're now out of your home

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.530
and making poor decisions
all on their own.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:53.850
- That you don't know about anymore.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:54.683
they're not at home.

00:08:54.683 --> 00:08:56.100
- And you know, you know
what, don't tell me.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.530
Unless I need to know or
you need to get out of jail.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.660
So is it true that you
once scared them though

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:01.743
on a camping trip?

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.740
'Cause this is traumatic.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.400
- Yeah, so we went camping.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.850
They were probably eight and six,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.250
and I would always tell
them stories about Bigfoot.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.830
And so I decided we were
going to the Pacific Northwest

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.600
where Bigfoot supposedly lives.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.870
So I rented a really
elaborate Bigfoot costume

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.270
and I hid it in the Winnebago
that we were driving.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.880
And then I hopped out in
the middle of the night

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.340
and walked around the
campfire in the distance

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:31.410
making like horrible noises.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.080
It was really, it was so sick.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:34.913
It was the best.

00:09:34.913 --> 00:09:36.180
- My kids are six and eight.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.430
They would never sleep again.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.230
- Well one of 'em, Matthew,

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.503
came up and he kicked
me in the you know what.

00:09:43.485 --> 00:09:44.652
- That's bold.

00:09:45.543 --> 00:09:47.760
- This kid's gonna grow up to be somebody.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.130
- Yeah, what'd the other one do?

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.363
- He vomited.

00:09:51.363 --> 00:09:53.550
(laughing)

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.500
- It's like, no, they are in crisis.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:56.333
- That's right.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.230
Fight or flight.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.000
- Yeah, I love that one.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.800
And you said that costume was over, like.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.740
- Yeah, well the problem
was I hadn't tried it on.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.680
I just stupidly assumed it would fit.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:08.940
And so when I put it on

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.593
the eye holes were up here,

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:12.440
and I was like, ah!

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:15.780
- Probably looked more scary.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:16.710
- 'Cause I was staggering.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:19.812
- Yeah, until you were
kicked in that, by your son.

00:10:19.812 --> 00:10:20.820
(laughing)

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.760
- It was definitely a
family highlight for sure.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.170
- That's amazing oh my God.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:26.370
I'm afraid to scare my children

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.115
'cause I don't want them
to sleep with me forever.

00:10:28.115 --> 00:10:31.072
I already have to be like,
"Go to your own room."

00:10:31.072 --> 00:10:33.090
How did your wife feel when you told her

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.095
that you scared your
tiny children like that?

00:10:35.095 --> 00:10:36.813
Was she laughing or?

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:40.470
- I'm trying to think if
she was in on it or not.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:41.400
- Oh, okay.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.640
- I think I kind of told
her I was gonna do it.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.410
But I don't think she
really thought it through.

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.660
She's too busy doing mom things.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:49.888
And I think when it happened,

00:10:49.888 --> 00:10:53.384
my memory of it, she wasn't a fan.

00:10:53.384 --> 00:10:55.320
(laughing)

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.480
- It's 'cause she didn't
wanna sleep with 'em either.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:58.680
So we need another break.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.744
You can see Rob in Gog Gone on Netflix now

00:11:00.744 --> 00:11:04.773
and in 9-1-1 Lone Star, which
returns to Fox on January 24.

